fix-restart-syscall-no-chain rewrote restart_original_syscall() to poke
the registers and rewind the instruction pointer directly, and dropped
two side effects that the chained-syscall path it replaced still had.

Leaving restart_how unset makes the ptracer resume with whatever request
happens to be left over (PTRACE_CONT when seccomp is enabled, or the
last_restart_how that the PTRACE_EVENT_{EXIT,EXEC,FORK} cases reuse), so
the rewound wait* never gets a syscall-exit stop back: the ptracer runs
the syscall outside of PRoot's control and its bookkeeping falls behind,
which ends with the tracee dying of SIGSEGV.  This is the signature
seen in the bootstrap CI ("proot info: vpid 1: terminated with signal
11" right after nixos-rebuild-ng finishes the switch).

restore_original_regs has to go back to false for the same reason
chain_next_syscall() clears it: the sysexit stage would otherwise
restore the stale pre-syscall result over the one PRoot just wrote.

--- a/src/syscall/chain.c
+++ b/src/syscall/chain.c
@@ -186,6 +186,17 @@
 	instr_pointer = peek_reg(tracee, CURRENT, INSTR_POINTER);
 	poke_reg(tracee, INSTR_POINTER, instr_pointer - get_systrap_size(tracee));
 
+	/* Original register values must not be restored on top of the
+	 * result this syscall is about to produce (same as
+	 * chain_next_syscall()). */
+	tracee->restore_original_regs = false;
+
+	/* Break after the exit of this syscall, otherwise the rewound trap
+	 * is resumed with a stale request and never reports back.  This
+	 * mirrors chain_next_syscall(), which the chained-syscall path this
+	 * function replaced. */
+	tracee->restart_how = PTRACE_SYSCALL;
+
 	return 0;
 }
 
